          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            %strong Symplectic duality
            is the name of a hypothetical duality operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ↦
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0\mapsto \mathfrak{M}^!_0
            on conical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+singularities"} symplectic singularities
          %p
            The reader should be warned that this page and this circle of ideas are works in progress. At the moment, two papers by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Braden"} Braden
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Licata"} Licata
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Proudfoot"} Proudfoot
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Webster
            that will lay out lots of the ideas and details behind the claims made below are still in preparation; links will be added when they are ready to be publicly shared.
          %h2#correspondence_between_geometric_data Correspondence between geometric data
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            a conical symplectic singularity, there is an associated
            %span.newWikiWord
              Harrison homology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Harrison+homology"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi HP
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HP^2( \mathfrak{M}_0)
            which is the base of a universal
            %span.newWikiWord
              Poisson deformation
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Poisson+deformation"} ?
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coxeter+group"} Coxeter group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            (called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+group"} Weyl group
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔔
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{Q}
            %span.newWikiWord
              factorial
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Q-factorial"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              terminalization
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/terminal+singularity"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+resolution"} symplectic resolution
            is an example of such a terminalization, but not all symplectic singularities have resolutions). Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{\mathbb{Z}}
            denote the Picard group of this variety.
          %p
            The variety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            also has a finite-dimensional group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
            commuting with the conical structure. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %span.newWikiWord
              Levi complement
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Levi+complement"} ?
            of this group,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+torus"} maximal torus
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕎
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{W}=N_G(T)/T
            its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+group"} Weyl group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^!
          %p
            Among the basic properties we expect of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            is
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture Conjecture
            %p
              There are canonical isomorphisms of Coxeter groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕎
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{W}\cong W^!
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕎
                      %mo !
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{W}^!\cong W
              and canonical equivariant isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔱
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{t}\cong H^!
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔱
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{t}^!\cong H
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi HP
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔐
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔱
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝕎
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≅
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi G
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HP^2( \mathfrak{M}_0)\cong\mathfrak{t}^!/\mathbb{W}^!\cong \mathfrak{g}^!/G^!
          %h2#quantizations Quantizations
          %p
            Every conical symplectic singularity has a canonical
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of filtered non-commutative algebras over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi HP
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HP^2( \mathfrak{M}_0)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+graded"} associated graded
            is isomorphic to the
            %span.newWikiWord
              coordinate ring
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/coordinate+ring"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}[\mathfrak{M}_0]
          %p
            Using the isomorphisms above, the choice of a quantization parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            thus corresponds to a
            = succeed "-conjugacy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^!
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^!
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0^!
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi\in A_\lambda
            which corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            of this vector field under associated graded, and thus whose inner action on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\lambda
            is a lift of that of the vector field on functions on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo ;
                        %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda;\xi}
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
              is the category of finitely generated
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\lambda
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
              acts locally finitely with generalized eigenvalues whose real parts bounded above.
          %p
            Note that the definition of category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            involved the choice of parameters
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_2 Conjecture
            %p
              There is a choice of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"}> b-fields
              ”
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi b
                      %mo !
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in H, b^!\in H^!
              such that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi λ
                      %mo !
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda\in H_{\mathbb{Z}}, \lambda^!\in H_{\mathbb{Z}}^!
              generic, with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ξ
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi,\xi^!
              the elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^!
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              associated to these elements as described above, the categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mo ;
                        %msup
                          %mi ξ
                          %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda+b;\xi^!}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi λ
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %msup
                          %mi b
                          %mo !
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ξ
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda^!+b^!,\xi}^!
              generate equivalent triangulated subcategories of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated categories
              of
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi λ
                          %mo +
                          %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\lambda+b}
              = succeed "-modules." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi λ
                            %mo !
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %msup
                            %mi b
                            %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\lambda^!+b^!}
          %p
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/51289/"} This mathoverflow question
            was an attempt to get a rigorous definition of what these b-fields are.
          %h2#the_smooth_case The smooth case
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_3 Conjecture
            %p
              The variety
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
              possesses a symplectic resolution of singularities
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔐
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^!
              acts on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mn 0
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0^!
              with a unique fixed point.
          %p
            Thus, if both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mn 0
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}_0^!
            possess symplectic resolutions, they also both carry Hamiltonian torus actions with isolated fixed points. In this case, the simple modules in category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ;
                      %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda;\xi}
            (for both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            generic) are in bijection with
            = succeed "-fixed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔐
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_4 Conjecture
            %p
              The categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mo ;
                        %msup
                          %mi ξ
                          %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda+b;\xi^!}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi λ
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %msup
                          %mi b
                          %mo !
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ξ
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda^!+b^!,\xi}^!
              are both
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+algebra"} Koszul
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+dual"} Koszul dual
              to each other.
          %p Underlying this Koszul duality should be a bijection between fixed points. This bijection should also appear on the level of equivariant cohomology.
          %p
            The map of equivariant homology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} scqup
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mi T
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔐
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \scqup{x\in \mathfrak{M}^T} H_2^T(\{x\})\to H_2^T(\mathfrak{M})
            produces an arrangement of subspaces in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_2^T(\mathfrak{M})
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_5 Conjecture
            %p
              The vector spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_2^T(\mathfrak{M})
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_2^{T^!}(\mathfrak{M}^!)
              are canonically dual, with the spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_2^T(\{x\})
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_2^{T^!}(\{x^!\})
              mutual annihilators.
          %p In the paper
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Braden"}> Tom Braden
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Licata"} Anthony Licata
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Proudfoot"} Nicholas Proudfoot
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Ben Webster
              = succeed "," do
                %em Localization algebras and deformations of Koszul algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 17
              %a{:href => "http://front.math.ucdavis.edu/arXiv:0905.1335"} arXiv:arXiv:0905.1335
          %p it’s shown that Koszul algebras have a similar duality property when equivariant cohomology rings are replaced with the centers of certain deformations.
          %h2#twisting_and_shuffling Twisting and shuffling
          %p
            The categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ;
                      %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda;\xi}
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            generic) carry actions of topologically defined groups. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔱
                    %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\subset \mathfrak{t}_{\mathbb{R}}
            be the subset of the Lie algebra of the compact real form of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            consisting of vector fields with isolated fixed points, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\subset H_{\mathbb{R}}
            be the complement of the walls of all the Mori chambers of the
            = succeed "-Picard" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(U/W,\lambda)
            by functors we call
            %strong twisting functors
            and on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝕎
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(V/\mathbb{W},\xi)
            by functors we call
            = succeed "." do
              %strong shuffling functors
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_6 Conjecture
            %p
              The Koszul duality between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mo ;
                        %msup
                          %mi ξ
                          %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda+b;\xi^!}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi λ
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %msup
                          %mi b
                          %mo !
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ξ
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\lambda^!+b^!,\xi}^!
              interchanges twisting and shuffling functors.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p All conjectured properties are proven for the following examples:
          %h3#hypertoric_varieties Hypertoric varieties
          %p
            For a
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                hypertoric variety
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/hypertoric+variety"} ?
            = succeed ")" do
              %em hypertoric enveloping algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Braden"} Braden
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Licata"} Licata
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Proudfoot"} Proudfoot
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Webster
            constructed Koszul dual categories conjectured to arise this way in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Braden"}> Tom Braden
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Licata"} Anthony Licata
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Proudfoot"} Nicholas Proudfoot
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Ben Webster
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gale duality and Koszul duality
              %strong 225
              %p
                (2010), no. 4, 2002–2049.
                %a{:href => "http://front.math.ucdavis.edu/0806.3256"} arXiv:0806.3256
          %p and then proved that these categories arise in geometry, compatibly with all conjectures discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Braden"}> Tom Braden
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Licata"} Anthony Licata
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Proudfoot"} Nicholas Proudfoot
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Ben Webster
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Hypertoric category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              %a{:href => "http://front.math.ucdavis.edu/1010.2001"} arXiv:1010.2001
          %h3#cotangent_bundles_and_s3varieties Cotangent bundles and S3-varieties
          %p
            For the cotangent bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*(G/B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a reductive algebraic group and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\subset G
            a Borel, the associated quantization is the universal enveloping algebra of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            is not actually the same as the BGG category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            it is vice versa. However, one can use a functor constructed by Soergel to construct an equivalence between these categories.
          %p
            The symplectic dual to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*(G^\vee/B^\vee)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^\vee
            is the Langlands dual of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p Modulo the issues discussed above, the Koszul duality portion of duality is shown in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"}> Alexander Beilinson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ginzburg"} Victor Ginzburg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Soergel"} Wolfgang Soergel
              %em Koszul
              %p
                duality patterns in representation theory_, J. Amer. Math. Soc.
                %strong 9
                (1996), no. 2, 473-527.
          %p and the duality between twisting and shuffling in
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Volodymyr Mazorchuk
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Volodymyr+Mazorchuk"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Serge Ovsienko
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Serge+Ovsienko"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Catharina+Stroppel"} Catharina Stroppel
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quadratic duals, Koszul dual functors, and applications
              %p
                %strong 361
                (2009), no. 3, 1129-1172.
          %p
            More generally, given partitions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %strong S3-variety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔛
                    %mi μ
                    %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{X}^\nu_\mu
            which is the preimage in the cotangent bundle of the space of flags of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            of the Slodowy slice to the nilpotent orbit with Jordan type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"}> Ben Webster
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Singular blocks of parabolic category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
                  and finite W-algebras
              = succeed ":12" do
                %strong 215
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.1860"} arXiv:0909.1860
          %p
            it’s shown that the category
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
          %p
            The dual is again an S3 variety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔛
                    %mi ν
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{X}^{\mu}_{\nu}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            have switched roles. The Koszul duality is that proven by BGS (referenced above), after using the equivalence to singular blocks of parabolic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            mentioned before. One particularly interesting special case of this is when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and the Slodowy slice to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_{\mu}
            in the full nilcone.
          %h3#quiver_varieties_and_affine_grassmannian_slices Quiver varieties and affine Grassmannian slices
          %p
            Perhaps the richest examples of symplectic singularities come from
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+varieties"} quiver varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+lattice"} weight lattice
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+algebra"} Kac-Moody algebra
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cO
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"}> Ben Webster
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Knot invariants and higher representation theory I: diagrammatic and geometric categorification of tensor products
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2020"} arXiv:1001.2020
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"}> Ben Webster
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Knot invariants and higher representation theory II: the categorification of quantum knot invariants
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1005.4559"} arXiv:1005.4559
          %p
            with the shuffling functors coinciding with the R-matrix action from the second paper above, and the twisting functors coinciding with the Chuang-Rouquier braid group action on the category (these statements will be proved in a forthcoming paper of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Ben Webster
          %p
            Heuristic considerations suggest that the dual variety to a quiver variety in finite type should be a slice between Schubert cells in the affine Grassmannian of the Langlands dual group. Ongoing work of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joel+Kamnitzer"} Joel Kamnitzer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"} Ben Webster
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oded+Yacobi"} Oded Yacobi
            is aimed at understanding the behavior of the quantization referred to above for an affine Grassmannian slice.
          %h2#connections_to_physics Connections to physics
          %p
            This topicis particularly speculative: the relationship of these constructions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            The physical theories which concern us are certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            in 3 dimensions which carry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            (see
            = succeed ");" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D3+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=3 super Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and a complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and are obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} dimensional reduction
            from 6 dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Seiberg"} Nathan Seiberg
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gauge dynamics and compactification to three dimensions
              %p
                Math. Phys., vol. 24, World Sci. Publ., River Edge, NJ, 1997, pp. 333–366. (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607163"}> arXiv:hep-th/9607163
                )
          %p
            The fields of this theory include scalar fermions valued in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+representation"} adjoint representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} mg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mg
            and hypermultiplets corresponding to the representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (called “the matter content”).
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ken+Intriligator"}> Ken Intriligator
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Seiberg"} Nathan Seiberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mirror symmetry in three-dimensional gauge theories
              %strong 387
              (1996), no. 3, 513–519 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607207"}> arXiv:hep-th/9607207
              )
          %p
            Seiberg and Intrilligator propose a notion of a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"}> mirror duality
            ” between such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+mirror+symmetry"}> 3d mirror symmetry
            ). The most important feature of such a duality is that it interchanges two spaces attached to them, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"} Higgs branch
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"} Coulomb branch
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            by the vanishing of some of the fields (the adjoint scalars for Higgs, the hypermultiplets for Coulomb). After arriving at a preliminary version of the conjectures discussed above, it was pointed out (initially by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Gukov"} Sergei Gukov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ALE+space"} ALE space
            forms a Higgs/Coulomb pair with the moduli space of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            of the corresponding simply-laced Lie group on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jan+de+Boer"}> Jan de Boer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kentaro+Hori"} Kentaro Hori
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hirosi+Ooguri"} Hirosi Ooguri
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yaron+Oz"} Yaron Oz
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mirror symmetry in three-dimensional gauge theories, quivers and D-branes
              %strong 493
              (1997), no. 1-2, 101–147.
          %p it is shown that
          %ul
            %li
              %p Gale dual hypertoric varieties are a Higgs/Coulomb pair.
            %li
              %p
                if an S3-variety is presented as a type A quiver variety, its dual is the dual S3-variety, also presented as a type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                quiver variety.
            %li
              %p all affine type quiver varieties are in a Higgs/Coulomb pair with another affine quiver variety, with the weight data transforming as in the rank/level duality.
          %p These examples strongly suggest that
          .num_conjecture
            %h6#conjecture_7 Conjecture
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔐
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"} Higgs branch
              of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D3+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=3 super Yang-Mills theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔐
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}^!
              exists and is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"} Coulomb branch
              of the same theory (equivalently, the Higgs branch of its
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror
          %p
            Unfortunately, this conjecture is not as useful as one might imagine, as the construction of the Coulomb branch is rather subtle, and we have found no description of it at the level of precision which would satisfy a mathematician. The issue is that while the “classical” and “quantum” Higgs branches are the same, the Coulomb branch acquires “quantum corrections.” The classical Coulomb branch is easy to describe; it is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*H/W
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is the abstract Cartan subalgebra of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔐
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{M}^!
            one must change the hyperkähler metric on this space, and thus also its topology, to account for the quantized nature of the situation. At the moment, we do not understand how to make these changes of metric and topology precise, but we are optimistic about the future.
          %h2#references References
          %p A recent seminar with a list of resources is
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.toronto.edu/dbutson/ls20.html"} GRT learning seminar winter 2020: Symplectic Duality
          %p A survey is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Proudfoot"}> Nick Proudfoot
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://pages.uoregon.edu/njp/research.pdf"} research statement
          %p A workshop dealing with related topics is
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.fields.utoronto.ca/programs/scientific/11-12/reptheory2012/"}> Young Researchers Workshop on Higher Algebraic and Geometric Structures: Modern Methods in Representation Theory
              , May 7-9, 2012 Hosted by the Fields Institute, at the Univerity of Toronto
        .revisedby
